Id: no-path-combine
Pattern: \bPath\.Combine\(
Subject: *.cs
Except: none
Issue: 450

A path here is composed with Path.Join and never with Path.Combine. The two
read the same on every call in this tree today and differ on exactly the call
that matters: Combine drops every earlier argument the moment a later one is
rooted, so a name that arrives rooted lands outside the directory the first
argument named and still writes successfully. Join never drops an argument. A
rooted later argument comes out under the earlier one, and whatever refuses it
sees a path inside the directory rather than a path somewhere else.

The plugin has one place a name it did not choose reaches a join, and that
place refuses a rooted name before the join is made, which is
CatalogueDirectory.DocumentPath. The rule is wider than that guard on purpose.
The forty-seven calls that stood when it landed were forty-three in the test
project, where no guard sits in front of any of them and where a temporary root
joined to a test's own literal is the ordinary shape; the C# analysis reported
thirty-three of them as cs/path-combine and read the other fourteen as the
same call. One method everywhere is what makes the property readable by a
pattern instead of by an argument per site, and it is what stops the scan
reporting the next test that joins a root to a literal.

The subject is every C# file rather than the two project directories by name,
because #14 renames the project directory and a rule naming today's path would
go quiet on the day of the rename instead of failing. The fixtures of the other
rules are inside that subject, so a fixture that composes a path composes it
with Join as well.

What it cannot do. It reads tracked text one line at a time, so a call through
an alias, a delegate or reflection is invisible to it. And Join settles only
which argument survives: a later argument carrying a relative step walks out of
the directory under either method, which is why DocumentPath refuses those
separately and why this rule does not claim to hold the location decision on
its own.
